// src/kernel/core/driver/process_death.pdx — R31.M0-1583 (#1583)
//
// The trigger. R29 built a complete cascade-restart and capability-
// revocation mechanism (restart.pdx, lifecycle.pdx, #1044/#1047/#1048) and
// nothing called it when a process died. This module is the correlation
// step that was missing: a pid goes in, and if that pid owned a driver row,
// the row's authority is torn down and the node is restarted.
//
// Design context
// --------------
// design/drivers/process-death.md is the full write-up. The three claims
// that shape this file:
//
// 1. This is leaked AUTHORITY, not leaked memory. A capability slot that
//    outlives its owner is inherited by whatever process next occupies it.
//    Leaked memory is recovered by reboot; leaked authority is exercised
//    first.
//
// 2. The fault path matters more than sys_exit. A process that calls exit
//    has usually finished its work and is the case LEAST likely to be
//    holding anything dangerous. The process that faults mid-transaction is
//    the one holding an OpRegion window or an I2C claim. Both call in here;
//    int/fault_kill.pdx is the arm that matters.
//
// 3. A pid is not an identity. pid_alloc is a dense-low-first scan and
//    pid_free zeroes the slot, so a reaped pid is handed to the very next
//    task_new. Correlating on the number alone would eventually revoke a
//    LIVE process's capabilities — worse than the leak being fixed. The key
//    is the pair (pid, generation); see §Two lines of defence below.
//
// Two lines of defence against pid reuse
// --------------------------------------
// FIRST, the binding is invalidated the moment it stops being true. It is
// dropped here before any teardown runs, dropped by
// driver_table_rearm_incarnation whenever a node is re-armed, and dropped by
// driver_table_unregister with the rest of the row. So a stale binding — a
// row naming a pid whose task is gone — is already a bug in the first line
// before the generation is ever consulted.
//
// SECOND, the row carries the low 16 bits of the owning task's incarnation
// counter, and the scan requires it to equal the generation currently
// occupying that pid slot. A stale binding that survives the first line is
// refused rather than acted on.
//
// Two independent mechanisms, because the failure they guard against is not
// symmetric with the failure they replace. Missing a revocation leaks
// authority the dead process held; performing the wrong one destroys
// authority a live process is using. The second is worse, so it gets the
// belt as well as the braces.
//
// Order of operations, and why
// ----------------------------
// driver_death_notify resolves the row, UNBINDS IT, and only then calls
// driver_restart_node. The unbind-first ordering is the whole of the
// idempotence argument: death, an explicit teardown and a supervisor sweep
// can all reach the same row, and the second arrival must resolve nothing
// and do nothing. If the unbind came last, a second call arriving while the
// first was mid-teardown would charge a second restart against the budget
// and drive the FSM through a life that no longer exists — which, five
// times over, latches perm_failed on a node whose only crime was dying once.
//
// The two axes this module now drives, and why they are two
// ---------------------------------------------------------
// R31.M1-1587 (#1587) closed the gap this header used to describe. It is
// worth keeping the distinction visible, because the two revocations that
// happen here answer different questions.
//
// OWNERSHIP (step 1.5). cap_owner_sweep_revoke(pid, gen) revokes every
// capability whose owner column names the dying task — its KIND_OP_REGION
// windows, KIND_I2C_BUS / KIND_I2C_SLAVE claims, KIND_GPIO_LINE pins and
// KIND_FW_SESSION handles — by walking the owner column, which is a
// relation that did not exist before #1587. It runs for EVERY dying
// process, before the driver-row lookup, because a crashed ACPI bubble
// holds all of those without necessarily occupying a driver row.
//
// LINEAGE (steps 2–5, unchanged). The driver row's own teardown revokes
// what the ROW names: the DMA domain at [+32] and every endpoint bound to
// the slot. Every cascade underneath keys on a PARENT identity — a cap
// slot, a bus row, a controller id.
//
// A capability can be DERIVED FROM a parent owned by X while being HELD BY
// Y, so neither sweep subsumes the other and neither is allowed to stand in
// for the other. They compose exactly once, inside cap_revoke_slot, which
// dispatches each owned slot to its kind's own audited revoke — so the
// owner sweep runs the lineage cascade beneath every slot it finds. See
// design/capabilities/ownership-and-lineage.md §1.
//
// What this module still does NOT do
// ----------------------------------
// It does not defend a stale HANDLE. A handle whose slot was revoked and
// re-minted resolves to the new capability; see cap/revoke.pdx §Stale
// handles. That is a handle-layout question, not a revocation one.

module ProcessDeath = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // Return codes
  //
  // Continue restart.pdx's DRV_RESTART_* block downward so a mixed error
  // trace still names its origin, and stay disjoint from it: a caller
  // holding a u64 from this module must never be able to mistake it for a
  // restart outcome.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let DRV_DEATH_OK          : u64 = 0

  // NOT AN ERROR. The overwhelmingly common answer: the task that died was
  // not a driver. Distinct from OK because a caller that wants to log
  // "a driver died" must not log it for every shell command that exits.
  pub let DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT     : u64 = 0xFFFFFFC1

  // The pid resolved to a row, but the restart machinery refused. The
  // underlying DRV_RESTART_* code is returned verbatim instead of this in
  // every real case — this exists so the constant block is complete and so
  // a future caller that collapses failures has a name to collapse to.
  pub let DRV_DEATH_ERR_RESTART : u64 = 0xFFFFFFC2

  // driver_death_bind refusals.
  pub let DRV_DEATH_BIND_OK     : u64 = 0
  pub let DRV_DEATH_BIND_NO_TASK: u64 = 0xFFFFFFC3   // pid hosts no live task
  pub let DRV_DEATH_BIND_REFUSED: u64 = 0xFFFFFFC4   // storage layer refused

  // driver_death_find_slot's "no row owns this task".
  pub let DRV_DEATH_SLOT_NONE   : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ============================================================================
  // driver_death_bind(slot, pid) -> u64
  //
  // The audited way to install a correlation key: read the pid's CURRENT
  // incarnation out of the scheduler and hand it to the storage layer.
  //
  // Refuses a pid with no live incarnation (generation 0), which is what
  // "pid_gen_read answers zero" means for both a never-allocated pid and an
  // out-of-range one. Binding a row to a pid that hosts nothing would
  // install a key that the next occupant of that pid slot would match — the
  // wrong-process revocation this whole design exists to prevent, created
  // at bind time instead of at death time.
  //
  // The supervisor calls this when it spawns a driver process. There is no
  // in-kernel supervisor yet, so today the callers are the boot witness and
  // the tests; that is a gap in WHO binds, not in whether death fires.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let driver_death_bind : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (slot: u64) (pid: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): install the (pid, generation) correlation key on a driver row, reading the generation from the scheduler rather than from the caller. Three pushes (rbx/r12/r13) keep rsp %16 == 0 across the two nested calls; rbx = slot, r12 = pid. Sequence: (1) pid_gen_read(pid) -> generation; zero means the pid hosts no live task (never allocated, already reaped, or out of range — pid_gen_read collapses all three deliberately) and is refused with BIND_NO_TASK rather than bound, because a key naming a pid that hosts nothing is a key the NEXT occupant of that pid slot will match, which manufactures the wrong-process revocation at bind time instead of at death time. (2) driver_table_bind_pid(slot, pid, gen); any non-zero storage-layer refusal (BAD_SLOT / BAD_PID / BAD_GEN) collapses to BIND_REFUSED, because from a supervisor's position they all mean the same thing — this row cannot be correlated — and the specific code has already been decided by gates the supervisor does not control. Deliberately NOT the same function as driver_table_bind_pid: keeping the generation lookup out of the storage layer preserves driver_table.pdx's zero-dependency posture, and is also what lets the witness manufacture a stale key by calling the storage primitive directly with a dead generation.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;                            // 3 pushes -> rsp %16 = 0

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // slot
        mov r12, rsi;                        // pid

        // (1) the pid must host a live incarnation.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call pid_gen_read;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  drv_death_bind_no_task;
        mov r13, rax;                        // generation

        // (2) install the key.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        mov rdx, r13;
        call driver_table_bind_pid;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne drv_death_bind_refused;

        xor rax, rax;                        // DRV_DEATH_BIND_OK
        jmp drv_death_bind_epilogue;

      drv_death_bind_no_task:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFC3;                 // DRV_DEATH_BIND_NO_TASK
        jmp drv_death_bind_epilogue;

      drv_death_bind_refused:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFC4;                 // DRV_DEATH_BIND_REFUSED

      drv_death_bind_epilogue:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // driver_death_find_slot(pid, gen) -> u64
  //
  // The correlation scan. Returns the slot whose row is bound to exactly
  // this (pid, generation), or DRV_DEATH_SLOT_NONE.
  //
  // Both halves of the key are compared, and BOTH comparisons are
  // load-bearing:
  //
  //   * pid alone would match a recycled pid — a row left naming pid 7 by a
  //     previous occupant, against an unrelated new task handed pid 7.
  //   * generation alone names nothing; generations are per-pid counters,
  //     so generation 3 of pid 7 and generation 3 of pid 8 are unrelated.
  //
  // The scan takes the generation as an argument rather than reading it,
  // so a caller that has already resolved the dying task's identity cannot
  // have it change underneath the scan, and so the recycled-pid case is
  // directly expressible in a test.
  //
  // First match wins. Two rows bound to the same live task would be a
  // supervisor bug — one driver process, one driver row — and returning the
  // first is the conservative answer: the second row keeps its binding and
  // is still reachable by the next sweep, whereas a "refuse on duplicate"
  // policy would revoke neither and leak both.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let driver_death_find_slot : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (pid: u64) (gen: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): linear scan of the 32 driver rows for the one bound to (pid, gen). Five pushes (rbx/r12/r13/r14/r15) keep rsp %16 == 0 across the three nested calls per iteration; rbx = pid, r12 = gen, r13 = cursor. Up-front refusals: pid 0 (the reserved sentinel, which every unbound row's pid field also reads as) and gen 0 (DRIVER_ROW_PIDGEN_UNBOUND, which every unbound row's generation field also reads as) both answer SLOT_NONE without scanning — without those two gates a caller passing zeros would match the first free row and revoke it. Per slot: driver_table_slot_in_use == 0 -> skip (a free row's key bytes are zero anyway, but the gate keeps the invariant local rather than relying on unregister's zeroing); driver_table_read_pid_gen != gen -> skip BEFORE reading the pid, because the generation is the discriminating half and checking it first makes the common no-match case one call cheaper; driver_table_read_pid != pid -> skip. First match returns its slot index. Both comparisons are required: pid alone matches a recycled pid against an unrelated new task, and a generation alone names nothing because generations are per-pid counters. Linear over 32 rows rather than a reverse index for the same reason driver_restart_reap_endpoints scans 128 endpoints — the table is the only storage that could be corrupted for the relation to break, and a side index could drift from it. Effects {mem} only; no audit emission here, so the scan stays callable from a probe that must not perturb the audit ring.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;                            // 5 pushes -> rsp %16 = 0

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // pid
        mov r12, rsi;                        // gen

        // A zero pid or a zero generation is what an UNBOUND row reads as.
        // Scanning for them would match the first free row.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je  drv_death_find_none;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  drv_death_find_none;

        xor r13, r13;                        // cursor

      drv_death_find_scan:
        cmp r13, 32;                         // DRIVER_TABLE_SLOTS
        jae drv_death_find_none;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call driver_table_slot_in_use;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  drv_death_find_next;

        // Generation first — it is the discriminating half, so the common
        // no-match case costs one call less.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
        cmp rax, r12;
        jne drv_death_find_next;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call driver_table_read_pid;
        cmp rax, rbx;
        jne drv_death_find_next;

        mov rax, r13;                        // found
        jmp drv_death_find_epilogue;

      drv_death_find_next:
        add r13, 1;
        jmp drv_death_find_scan;

      drv_death_find_none:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;         // DRV_DEATH_SLOT_NONE

      drv_death_find_epilogue:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // driver_death_notify(pid) -> u64
  //
  // THE entry point. A task with this pid has died — by exit or by fault.
  // Put the system back in a state where nothing it held is still live.
  //
  // Returns:
  //   DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT — the task owned no driver row. Not an error; this
  //                       is the answer for every process that is not a
  //                       driver, which is nearly all of them.
  //   DRV_DEATH_OK      — the row was torn down and the node re-armed.
  //   DRV_RESTART_*     — propagated VERBATIM from driver_restart_node, so
  //                       ESCALATED (budget exhausted, node left Stopped
  //                       and permanently failed) stays distinguishable
  //                       from a genuine failure. A crash loop that
  //                       escalates is the system working, not the system
  //                       breaking, and collapsing the two would hide it.
  //
  // Sequence:
  //   1. gen = pid_gen_read(pid). Zero -> NO_SLOT. This is the dying task's
  //      own incarnation: the pid slot is not freed until a parent reaps,
  //      so at death time the counter still names the process that is
  //      dying, not its successor.
  //   1.5 cap_owner_sweep_revoke(pid, gen) — THE OWNER AXIS. Runs for every
  //      dying process, before the row lookup, because the capabilities a
  //      process holds are not the capabilities its driver row names. Count
  //      discarded: this function's return value is a driver-row outcome.
  //   2. slot = driver_death_find_slot(pid, gen). None -> NO_SLOT.
  //   3. UNBIND THE ROW, before anything else touches it.
  //   4. Audit the correlation, while the row still exists to be named.
  //   5. driver_restart_node(slot), rc propagated verbatim.
  //
  // Step 3 before step 5 is the idempotence argument and it is not an
  // optimisation. Death, an explicit teardown and a supervisor sweep can
  // all reach the same row. With the unbind first, the second arrival
  // resolves nothing and returns NO_SLOT. With the unbind last — or with no
  // unbind at all — the second arrival would charge another restart against
  // the budget, and five arrivals would latch perm_failed on a node whose
  // only fault was dying once.
  //
  // Step 3 before step 4 keeps the audit record honest in the other
  // direction: the record says "this pid's binding was consumed", and it is
  // written after the consumption, so no record can describe an unbinding
  // that did not happen.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let driver_death_notify : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched} =
    fn (pid: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap, sched},
      justification: "R31.M0-1583 (#1583): the process-death trigger for the R29 cascade. Five pushes (rbx/r12/r13/r14/r15) put rsp at %16 == 0 for every nested call; rbx = pid, r12 = generation, r13 = slot. Sequence: (1) pid_gen_read(pid) — zero (never allocated, already reaped, or out of range) answers NO_SLOT without scanning; at death time this counter still names the DYING task, because pid_free runs at reap and not at exit, so the pid slot has not yet acquired a successor. (1.5) [R31.M1-1587 (#1587)] cap_owner_sweep_revoke(pid, gen) — THE OWNER AXIS, revoking every capability whose owner column names this incarnation. It runs BEFORE the row lookup and unconditionally, because it applies to every dying process and not only to registered drivers: a crashed ACPI bubble holds OpRegion windows, I2C claims and GPIO lines without necessarily occupying a driver row, so a sweep gated on find_slot succeeding would miss exactly the process #1086 P2 is about. Its count is DISCARDED: this function's return value is a driver-row outcome, and folding a capability count into it would make DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT mean two different things; the sweep emits its own audit record when it revoked anything. Placing it after step 1 rather than before is required — the sweep needs the generation, and a sweep keyed on the pid alone would revoke a live process's capabilities the moment that pid were recycled. (2) driver_death_find_slot(pid, gen) — SLOT_NONE answers NO_SLOT, which is the ordinary result for every process that is not a driver and is deliberately NOT an error code. (3) driver_table_unbind_pid(slot) BEFORE any teardown: this is the whole idempotence argument, not an optimisation. Death, an explicit teardown and a supervisor sweep can all reach one row; with the unbind first the second arrival resolves nothing, and with it last the second arrival charges another restart against the budget — five of which latch perm_failed on a node whose only fault was dying once. It also closes the pid-reuse window, since a pid recycled while the teardown runs can no longer resolve to this row. (4) drv_audit_emit(EV_RESTART=4, kind=0, subject=slot, principal=pid, outcome=2) AFTER the unbind so no record can describe an unbinding that did not happen; outcome 2 is a new discriminator in the existing event's outcome field — 0 is 'restarted by a supervisor', 1 is 'cascaded to a stop', 2 is 'the owning process died' — chosen over a new event id so the audit ring's existing decoder, its schema and its two consumers stay unchanged for what is a provenance distinction, not a new kind of thing happening. (5) driver_restart_node(slot), return value propagated VERBATIM so DRV_RESTART_ESCALATED stays distinguishable from a failure: a crash loop that escalates is the supervision policy working, and collapsing it into a generic error would hide exactly the signal an operator needs. Effects/caps are the union of the callees: {mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched}.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;                            // 5 pushes -> rsp %16 = 0

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // pid

        // ---- (1) the dying task's own incarnation ----
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call pid_gen_read;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  drv_death_no_slot;
        mov r12, rax;                        // generation

        // ---- (1.5) REVOKE EVERYTHING THIS PROCESS HELD ----
        //
        // Placed here, before the driver-row lookup, because it applies
        // to EVERY dying process and not only to registered drivers. A
        // crashed ACPI bubble holds OpRegion windows, I2C claims and
        // GPIO lines without necessarily occupying a driver row, and a
        // sweep that ran only after find_slot succeeded would miss
        // exactly the process #1086 P2 is about.
        //
        // The count is not folded into this function's RETURN value:
        // driver_death_notify's return is a DRIVER-ROW outcome, and
        // folding a capability count into it would make
        // DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT mean two different things. The sweep emits
        // its own audit record when it revoked anything.
        //
        // R31.M2-1590 (#1590): it is, however, no longer DISCARDED. It
        // is handed to boot_spawn_note_death together with the (pid,
        // generation) key, which stays silent for every task that is
        // not a boot-spawned service and, for one that is, asserts the
        // count is non-zero. That assertion is the only thing standing
        // between "the sweep ran" and "the sweep found what it was
        // supposed to find": a sweep against CAP_OWNER_NONE returns 0
        // before it reads a single slot, so with capabilities seeded
        // but never claimed this call site would have reported a clean
        // teardown over a live leak. See boot_spawn.pdx §note_death.
        mov rdi, rbx;                        // pid
        mov rsi, r12;                        // generation
        call cap_owner_sweep_revoke;

        // rax = matched-slot count. rbx (pid) and r12 (generation) are
        // callee-save-preserved across the sweep by its own 5-push
        // prologue, so both are still live for the observer below.
        mov rdx, rax;                        // sweep_count
        mov rdi, rbx;                        // pid
        mov rsi, r12;                        // generation
        call boot_spawn_note_death;

        // ---- (2) which row did it own? ----
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call driver_death_find_slot;
        mov r14, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;         // DRV_DEATH_SLOT_NONE
        cmp rax, r14;
        je  drv_death_no_slot;
        mov r13, rax;                        // slot

        // ---- (3) consume the binding FIRST ----
        mov rdi, r13;
        call driver_table_unbind_pid;

        // ---- (4) record the correlation, after consuming it ----
        mov rdi, 4;                          // DRV_AUDIT_EV_RESTART
        xor rsi, rsi;                        // kind = 0: not a cap event
        mov rdx, r13;                        // subject   = driver slot
        mov rcx, rbx;                        // principal = the dead pid
        mov r8,  2;                          // outcome 2 = owning process died
        call drv_audit_emit;

        // ---- (5) the R29 cascade ----
        mov rdi, r13;
        call driver_restart_node;

        // ---- (5.5) R31.M2-1600 (#1600): the RESPAWN arm. ----
        //
        // driver_restart_node re-armed the row's incarnation and
        // returned. That closed the SUPERVISION question ("does this row
        // hold a fresh incarnation ready to bind?") and left open the
        // PROCESS question ("is there a userspace task holding it?").
        // Until #1600 there was no answer to the second question; the row
        // was re-armed and no server ran, which is exactly the observation
        // #1600 is filed against.
        //
        // Gated on DRV_RESTART_OK (0), not on "any success", because
        // DRV_RESTART_ESCALATED intentionally leaves the row Stopped with
        // perm_failed latched -- a crash loop that escalates is the
        // supervision policy working, and manufacturing a new process
        // against a permanently-failed row would erase the answer. Every
        // other outcome (ERR_BAD_SLOT, ERR_PERM_FAILED, ERR_STOP,
        // ERR_TEARDOWN, ERR_REARM) means the row is not in a state that
        // could carry a new incarnation, and any of them being handed a
        // fresh pid to bind against would install the same key twice.
        cmp rax, 0;                          // DRV_RESTART_OK
        jne drv_death_epilogue;              // propagate verbatim

        // boot_spawn_respawn is silent for a task that is not a boot-
        // spawned service -- it consults the loader's own registry and
        // returns 0 for any (pid, gen) it does not recognise -- so this
        // call is safe to make unconditionally for every driver row that
        // re-armed cleanly. In particular a driver installed via the
        // R29.M3 registry v2 rather than boot_spawn_user_task will land
        // here and get 0 back; the driver row was already re-armed and
        // that is the whole of the answer for such a driver at R31.M2.
        mov rdi, rbx;                        // dying pid
        mov rsi, r12;                        // dying generation
        call boot_spawn_respawn;             // rax = new pid | 0
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  drv_death_epilogue_ok;           // nothing to bind or record

        mov r14, rax;                        // r14 = new pid (survives the bind)

        // Rebind the row to the fresh incarnation. Runs BEFORE the audit
        // so a record that names the new pid cannot precede the state
        // that makes the name meaningful. driver_death_bind reads the
        // fresh generation out of the scheduler itself, so this call is
        // the mirror image of the initial bind the supervisor performed
        // on the original process -- with the same refusal shape (a pid
        // that hosts no live task collapses to BIND_NO_TASK), which
        // cannot occur here in the normal path because boot_spawn_respawn
        // published the pid via task_new + runq_enqueue before returning
        // it. The rc is discarded because there is no caller left to
        // refuse to: the audit line below records the rebind as observed.
        mov rdi, r13;                        // slot
        mov rsi, r14;                        // new pid
        call driver_death_bind;

        // Audit the respawn with a fresh outcome value. The alternatives
        // considered:
        //   * a new EV_ id: adds a schema entry and a decoder path for a
        //     provenance distinction on an existing event, which is what
        //     the outcome field is FOR;
        //   * reusing outcome 0 ("restarted by a supervisor"): loses the
        //     distinction between "the row was re-armed" and "the row was
        //     re-armed AND a fresh process took its place", and #1600
        //     exists specifically to make that distinction observable.
        // Outcome 4 continues the enumeration from process_death.pdx's
        // own outcome 2 ("owning process died"): 0..2 are the pre-#1600
        // outcomes, 4 is the post-#1600 respawn record. Principal carries
        // the new pid so a consumer of the audit ring can correlate the
        // rebind with the driver row named in subject.
        mov rdi, 4;                          // DRV_AUDIT_EV_RESTART
        xor rsi, rsi;                        // kind = 0: not a cap event
        mov rdx, r13;                        // subject   = driver slot
        mov rcx, r14;                        // principal = new pid
        mov r8,  4;                          // outcome 4 = respawned
        call drv_audit_emit;

      drv_death_epilogue_ok:
        xor rax, rax;                        // DRV_DEATH_OK
        jmp drv_death_epilogue;

      drv_death_no_slot:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFC1;                 // DRV_DEATH_NO_SLOT

      drv_death_epilogue:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
